{"candidates":[{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The government maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022, targeting a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"context","actor":"The government","action":"maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance","mechanism":"Targeted a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","outcome":"Kept fiscal policy neutral while the economy recovered.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["3.8 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"The government has maintained a broadly neutral fiscal stance in 2022, targeting a fiscal deficit of 3.8 percent of GDP.","locator":"page 2","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["IMF staff appraisal, not an independent audit.","Summarizes the authorities' stance rather than the full execution detail."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.3"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The authorities used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost of living, offsetting windfall gains from oil revenues.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"mixed","actor":"The authorities","action":"used largely untargeted subsidies","mechanism":"Subsidies were used to cushion living costs and were offset by oil-revenue windfalls.","outcome":"Mitigated inflation pressure, but through broad subsidies rather than targeted support.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["around 2 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"It has used largely untargeted subsidies to mitigate the rise in the cost of living, offsetting the windfall gains from oil revenues.","locator":"page 2","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["IMF staff characterization.","Does not by itself show household-level incidence or targeting precision."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The authorities smoothed retail fuel prices and stabilized the prices of some essential food items, including a nonbinding agreement on 24 items for six months.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"mixed","actor":"The authorities","action":"smoothed retail fuel prices and stabilized some essential food prices","mechanism":"Fuel subsidies, a nonbinding agreement with producers and distributors, temporary tariff reductions, and steps to reduce distribution costs and promote food production were used.","outcome":"Lowered fuel and food cost pressures in 2022.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["24 items","six months"],"excerpt":"As global oil prices have risen, the authorities have smoothed retail fuel prices and took steps to stabilize prices of some essential food items. In a nonbinding agreement, several food producers and distributors agreed to stabilize the prices of 24 items initially for six months. In addition, the authorities provided a temporary tariff reduction on certain foods, while taking steps to reduce distribution costs and promote food production, especially by small farmers.","locator":"page 12","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 inflation-response period.","source_limitations":["IMF staff summary, not a direct administrative order.","No direct household incidence estimate is given in the excerpt."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The 2023 draft budget increased spending to accommodate higher interest payments, social pensions for the elderly, priority infrastructure projects, and selected rural support programs.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The authorities","action":"raised planned expenditure in the draft budget","mechanism":"Allocated more nominal spending to interest, social pensions, infrastructure, fertilizers, national milk procurement, and rural supplies.","outcome":"Expanded planned public spending for social programs and infrastructure relative to the 2022 outturn.","dates":["2023 draft budget","2022 outturn"],"quantities":["8.5 percent","0.1 percentage points of authorities' projected GDP"],"excerpt":"Compared to the authorities estimated 2022 outturn, expenditure in 2023 is increased by 8.5 percent in nominal terms (or by 0.1 percentage points of authorities’ projected GDP). This increase accommodates higher spending of interest payments, some social programs—particularly (non-contributory) social pensions for the elderly—and priority infrastructure projects. In addition, the allotted funding for social programs related to fertilizers, procurement of national milk, and rural supplies has also been increased, though the size of the programs remains modest.","locator":"page 13","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Indirectly relevant to 2022 because it describes the immediately following budget cycle.","source_limitations":["This is a draft budget proposal described by IMF staff, not executed spending.","The excerpt does not break out implementation quality by project."],"question_ids":["5B.1","5B.6"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Retail fuel price stabilization came at a sizeable budgetary cost and disproportionately benefited higher income households.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"adverse","actor":"Retail fuel price stabilization","action":"lowered fuel prices through subsidy support","mechanism":"Fuel subsidies reduced retail prices and were financed through the budget.","outcome":"The measure cost about 1.5 percent of GDP and favored higher-income households more than lower-income households.","dates":["2022"],"quantities":["1.5 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"The measure has come at a sizeable budgetary cost, estimated at around 1.5 percent of GDP, and has disproportionately benefited higher income households.","locator":"page 19","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["IMF staff estimate, not a household survey.","The excerpt does not show the full distributional breakdown."],"question_ids":["5B.8","5B.9"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"LAW-005","claim":"For 10 to 16 December 2022, the fuel stimulus was set at 26.96 percent for gasoline under 91 octanes and 59.77 percent for diesel, with a zero stimulus for premium gasoline and non-fossil fuels.","claim_type":"legal_status","polarity":"mixed","actor":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público","action":"set the fuel excise stimulus percentages and amounts","mechanism":"Adjusted the IEPS stimulus schedule and the per-liter quotas for the specified week.","outcome":"Reduced fuel taxes for the stated period.","dates":["10 al 16 de diciembre de 2022","08 de diciembre de 2022"],"quantities":["26.96%","59.77%","$1.4803","$3.6071","$4.0114","$4.6375","$2.4283"],"excerpt":"Gasolina menor a 91 octanos 26.96%
Gasolina mayor o igual a 91 octanos y combustibles no fósiles 0.00%
Diésel 59.77%","locator":"extracted HTML block 2","attribution":"Secretaría de Hacienda y Crédito Público, Acuerdo 173/2022","period_fit":"Directly within the target year 2022.","source_limitations":["The order states scheduled tax treatment, not realized pass-through to retail prices.","It does not by itself measure who benefited from the stimulus."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.9"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"In April 2021 the authorities enacted new legislation restricting subcontracting of the workforce to prevent violations of labor law and tax evasion.","claim_type":"legal_status","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The authorities","action":"enacted new legislation restricting subcontracting","mechanism":"The law banned service firms from providing employees to be supervised and managed by a third party, tightened registration and reporting requirements, and made non-compliance a criminal offense.","outcome":"A sizeable share of subcontracted workers later shifted into direct employment.","dates":["April 2021"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"In April 2021, the authorities enacted new legislation restricting the subcontracting of the workforce, with the aim of preventing the violation of labor law and tax evasion. The new law bans service firms from providing employees to be supervised and managed by a third-party, while it still allows subcontracting of services if the employees are monitored by the firm providing the service. The legislation also tightened registration and reporting requirements. Non-compliance with this new subcontracting law became a criminal offence.","locator":"page 69","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Antecedent reform with 2022 relevance.","source_limitations":["The excerpt describes the law and intended purpose, not a full enforcement audit.","The outcome is only indirectly evidenced in the same annex."],"question_ids":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.8"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"The subcontracting reform was associated with large shifts from subcontracted to direct employment and no apparent negative impact on aggregate employment.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The subcontracting reform","action":"changed employment classification patterns","mechanism":"Workers moved from subcontracted status to direct employment in manufacturing and retail trade.","outcome":"Subcontracted employment fell sharply while aggregate employment did not appear to suffer.","dates":["second quarter of 2021","third quarter of 2021","first quarter of 2021"],"quantities":["87 percent","70 percent"],"excerpt":"In the manufacturing and retail trade sectors, for example, subcontracted employment was reduced by 87 percent and 70 percent, respectively, compared to the first quarter of 2021. At the same time, there was no apparent change in total employment in these sectors by the end of the third quarter, suggesting that most of the previously subcontracted workers were rehired as direct employees.","locator":"page 70","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Antecedent reform with 2022 relevance.","source_limitations":["Sector-specific annex analysis, not a nationwide audit.","The annex notes measurement issues around value added and employment classification."],"question_ids":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.8"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Under the current administration, revenue mobilization centered on improving tax compliance through anti-fraud and anti-avoidance reforms.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"The current administration","action":"improved tax compliance administration","mechanism":"Abolished offsetting excess tax credits against other taxes, required digital platforms to withhold taxes, implemented GAAR, prohibited company mergers for tax purposes, and adopted OECD BEPS actions.","outcome":"Income, corporate, and VAT revenues grew as shares of GDP between 2019 and 2022, and the IMF cites a reduction in VAT evasion of around MEX$170 billion between 2018 and 2021.","dates":["since 2019","2018 to 2021","2019 to 2022"],"quantities":["MEX$170 billion","1.0 percent of GDP","0.6 percent of GDP"],"excerpt":"Under the current administration, revenue mobilization efforts in Mexico have centered on improving tax compliance. Notable reforms since 2019 have included:
• The abolition of the right to offset excess tax credits against other taxes, building on staff recommendations regarding tackling large-scale tax fraud;
• The requirement that digital platforms withhold taxes due on transactions in which they acted as intermediary;
• Implementation of General Anti-Abuse Rules (GAAR) and the prohibition of company mergers for tax purposes to tackle corporate tax planning; and
• Enaction of actions 2, 4 and 12 in the OECD’s Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) agenda.","locator":"page 90","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly relevant to 2022 fiscal administration and its recent antecedents.","source_limitations":["The revenue effects are IMF staff-attributed and may combine several reforms.","This is not a single enforcement case against a politically connected actor."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Mexico's economic data were considered timely and adequate for surveillance, and the country subscribes to the SDDS.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"favorable","actor":"Mexico","action":"published economic data and subscribed to IMF dissemination standards","mechanism":"Provided comprehensive economic data on a timely basis under the SDDS framework.","outcome":"The IMF judged the data adequate for surveillance.","dates":["as of August 31, 2022"],"quantities":[],"excerpt":"Mexico has accepted the obligations of Article VIII, sections 2, 3, and 4. Comprehensive economic data are available for Mexico on a timely basis and economic data are adequate for surveillance. It subscribes to the SDDS.","locator":"page 97","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 surveillance period.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF surveillance assessment, not a measurement of all data quality dimensions.","It does not evaluate every dataset or statistical series individually."],"question_ids":["5B.7"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false},{"source_id":"MAC-010","claim":"Mexico's public debt was expected to remain broadly stable in the medium term and then decline over the extended horizon.","claim_type":"source_assertion","polarity":"context","actor":"Mexico's public sector","action":"followed a projected debt path","mechanism":"Debt dynamics were expected to be offset by fiscal surpluses and then ease over the longer horizon.","outcome":"Debt was judged sustainable with high probability over the extended time horizon.","dates":["2022","2027","2031"],"quantities":["57.6 percent of GDP","59.3 percent of GDP","high probability"],"excerpt":"The projected debt path and GFN are expected to increase somewhat over the main projection period but decline over the extended 10-year period. Debt is therefore judged to be sustainable with high probability over the extended time horizon.","locator":"page 60","attribution":"International Monetary Fund, 2022 Article IV Consultation with Mexico","period_fit":"Medium-term and longer-run trajectory relevant to the 2022 assessment.","source_limitations":["This is an IMF projection and risk assessment, not an ex post outcome.","It reflects model-based debt dynamics under stated assumptions."],"question_ids":["5B.3","5B.10"],"verification_status":"pending","verification_notes":[],"premium_verification_required":false}],"rejected_or_omitted_passages":[],"unresolved_gaps":["No direct 2022 evidence in the supplied packet for 5B.2 on appointment, independence, or performance-based retention of qualified economic professionals."]}
